# Cross-platform probe — the documented install path on a machine that is not macOS
#
#   DEEPBLEND_BLENDER_TARBALL_DIR=<a dir holding blender-5.2.1-linux-x64.tar.xz> \
#     node deepblend/tools/cross-platform-probe.mjs | tee deepblend/docs/probe-cross-platform.log
#
# Taken 2026-09-23 (commercial-readiness ledger C5). What it is evidence for: `install.md` §0's
# sentence to every non-macOS user — "install Blender 5.2.1 yourself, then set `blenderPath` on the
# deepblend-blender-runtime row of the operator layer" — and the four documented steps around it.
#
# WHY IT NEEDS A CONTAINER, AND WHY THAT IS ALLOWED. C5 asks what a user on another platform actually
# experiences, and there is no other way to read that from a macOS host. One container is started, the
# documented steps run inside it, and it is destroyed; the repository is copied in through a read-only
# bind mount and the container's `$DSH_HOME` is its own. The rule this repository follows — do not add
# a second process unless a row's criterion requires it, and write the reason down — is satisfied by
# that sentence and by this one.
#
# IT IS x86_64 ON PURPOSE. Blender 5.2.1 publishes `blender-5.2.1-linux-x64.tar.xz` and NO Linux arm64
# build at all (checked against the upstream directory listing for 5.2, 5.1, 4.5 and 4.2 — every one
# of them offers only `linux-x64`), so an arm64 container, which this host could run natively, cannot
# hold the Blender the product pins. The tarball is downloaded once on the host and mounted, so the
# container's own network is used only for apt and for npm.
#
# HOW TO READ IT. Sections 0-8 are the path in order; "the readings" at the end is what the verdict is
# derived from. Two lines in it are NOTES rather than problems, and both say why:
#
#   contract layer   this container runs as root and has its own TMPDIR, so a handful of cases that
#                    read file permissions or the checkout differ from a CI runner. CI runs this same
#                    layer on ubuntu-latest and is the authority for it — it is green there.
#   render note      the fixture render hits the product's own 180 s bootstrap deadline, because an
#                    x86_64 Blender under qemu on an arm64 host is far slower than any real machine.
#                    The Blender PROBE above it is the reading that matters: 14/15, the only failed
#                    check being "GPU device detected", which is the container having no GPU.
#
# The verdict line is derived from those readings rather than written by hand (D199). It exits 0, 1 or
# 2: 0 = every product claim held, 1 = a claim did not, 2 = the container could not be prepared — and
# that third code exists because the first version of this probe reported a broken npm install as four
# product failures.

